+    <p id="fbReason">This 3D exhibit needs WebGL, which is unavailable in this browser.</p>
+    <p><b>The idea in brief:</b> an air conditioner moves heat instead of making cold. Refrigerant circulates in a sealed copper loop. In <b>Cooling</b>, the cold indoor coil absorbs heat from room air; the compressor makes the refrigerant hotter than outdoor air, so the outdoor coil can release that heat outside. An expansion device drops the pressure so the refrigerant becomes cold again.</p>
+    <p>In <b>Heating</b>, a four-way reversing valve swaps the coils' roles: the outdoor coil becomes colder than winter air and absorbs heat, and the indoor coil releases it into the room. Indoor and outdoor air never mix. A <b>split system</b> uses two units joined by refrigerant lines; a <b>monoblock</b> keeps the whole circuit indoors and ducts outdoor air through the wall.</p>
